  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Canadian wildfire smoke will continue to cause poor \nair quality today.\n\nSmoke from Canadian wildfires extends into western NY this morning. \nA warm front will move across the region today, and smoke will lift \nback northward. Based on the latest HRRR and REFS, surface smoke is \nexpected to improve across western NY late this morning through \nafternoon, and by evening across north-central NY. Air Quality \nAlerts remain in effect today.\n\nKey Message 2...Showers and thunderstorms are expected at times \nthrough this evening, with numerous severe thunderstorms able to \nproduce damaging winds and locally heavy rain. \n\nWV imagery shows a potent shortwave trough digging into the Upper \nGreat Lakes region this morning. The trough will continue to dig \ninto the Lower Great Lakes region, with a surface low deepening as \nit moves from Lake Superior to Quebec today. Isentropic lift and \nmoisture advection ahead of a warm front has led to showers across \nthe Lake Ontario region this morning. A few thunderstorms can't be \nruled out in this activity. The warm front will move across the \nregion this morning. A tight moisture gradient and elevated \ninstability will support scattered showers and a few thunderstorms \nacross western NY, mainly staying south of I-90 through mid-morning. \nThe chance for showers and a few thunderstorms will increase east of \nLake Ontario with the warm front late this morning. There will be \nsome dry time across western NY mid/late morning. \n\nThe region will be get into the warm sector with dewpoints climbing \ninto the upper 60s to lower 70s today. The surface low will pass by \nto the north while an associated cold front drops south across the \nregion late this afternoon through this evening. A favorable set-up \nfor severe weather is expected across the forecast area today. While \nsmoke will linger today, heating within a moist airmass will result \nin destabilization. The wind field will strengthen ahead of the cold \nfront resulting in 30-40knots of 0-6km shear. A broken line of \nstorms is expected to form on a pre-frontal trough or differential \nheating boundaries as early as noon, with a secondary line of \nconvection with the front between 2pm-8pm. Numerous severe \nthunderstorms are expected today with damaging winds, locally heavy \nrainfall, and an isolated tornado as the main threats. The SPC has \nissued a Enhanced Risk (3 of 5) of severe thunderstorms.\n\nThe threat for thunderstorms will come to an end behind the cold \nfront tonight. A few showers may linger into Sunday morning.\n\nKey Message 3...Gusty winds are expected northeast of Lake Erie this \nafternoon.\n\nThe pressure gradient ahead of the surface low and cold front will \ntighten and southwest winds will increase across the region. A 30-40 \nknot low level jet will move overhead. Wind gusts of 25-35 mph are \nexpected this afternoon, with the strongest wind gusts northeast of \nLake Erie. A few gusts of up to 40 mph area possible northeast of \nLake Erie. The timing of the strongest wind gusts outside of \nthunderstorms is noon-4PM.",
